The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (min_gate_len[tc] > gate_len[tc])
    min_gate_len[tc] = gate_len[tc];
```
```c
gate_len[tc] = 0;
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the logic treats gate close events as level-triggered. When a gate is closed (i.e., a 0 for that traffic class) and `gate_len[tc]` is 0 (indicating it was never open), the condition `min_gate_len[tc] > gate_len[tc]` evaluates to true, leading to `min_gate_len[tc]` being incorrectly set to 0. This prevents it from tracking any subsequent open intervals correctly.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (min_gate_len[tc] > gate_len[tc] && gate_len[tc])
    min_gate_len[tc] = gate_len[tc];
```
```c
for (tc = 0; tc < OCELOT_NUM_TC; tc++)
    if (!(gates_ever_opened & BIT(tc)))
        min_gate_len[tc] = 0;
```
Reason:
The fix introduces an additional check `&& gate_len[tc]` to ensure that `min_gate_len[tc]` is only updated when `gate_len[tc]` is greater than 0, effectively making the update edge-triggered. Additionally, the loop at the end sets `min_gate_len[tc]` to 0 for traffic classes that have never had their gates opened, correctly distinguishing between permanently closed and open gates.

